  "Interpretable-feature steering is judged almost entirely by whether a target-property predictor improves. We hypothesize a distinct, currently-unmeasured failure: steering an interpretable feature to control a property silently drags generation into regions where the model's OWN representation becomes unreliable (its embedding drifts toward random-like, low-information vectors), because steering displaces activations off the training manifold along directions the feature was never calibrated for -- so the apparent property gain is least trustworthy exactly where steering is strongest, even when the finished object is valid and satisfies the predictor. We further hypothesize that a cheap, intervention-free, pre-registered representation-reliability score declines monotonically with steering strength beyond what a matched-OOD-but-unsteered control explains, predicts per-generation whether the claimed property is real versus illusory, and can gate steering to keep only trustworthy designs -- an axis existing property-predictor-plus-validity evaluation misses.",
