# ===================== idx 16 steering_sideeffect_law =======================
edits[16] = {
 "Abstract":
  "Steering an interpretable feature to control one property of a generated protein or molecule is the standard controllable-design paradigm, judged mainly by whether the target property moves. In text language models, steering is known to cause off-target effects, and SAE-based methods (e.g., Chalnev et al. 2024) minimize them using EMPIRICALLY-MEASURED effect vectors; but there the off-target 'property' is a fuzzy behavior and the effect must be measured per target. We make two moves. First, a new SETTING: in scientific generative models every off-target property (helix content, charge, logP, ring count, motif presence) is an objectively verifiable structural fact, so collateral drift becomes a measurable VECTOR, not a benchmark wobble. Second, a sharper QUESTION: can that drift be predicted a priori, before any generation, from the SAE dictionary geometry alone? We hypothesize each feature carries a fixed 'side-effect signature' -- computable from decoder-direction overlaps and a per-feature property-Jacobian -- that forecasts its drift vector. We frame this as a domain- and battery-specific empirical regularity, not a universal law. Using ESM-2 and a SMILES chemical LM with sparse autoencoders, we test three separable hypotheses: (1) single-target steering induces structured, non-random off-target drift under hard oracles; (2) the geometry-derived signature predicts each feature's measured drift vector, with a NO-SURROGATE decoder-overlap-only variant guarding against proxy leakage; and (3) the signature powers a pre-flight forecaster and a minimal-collateral controller (low-drift feature selection or orthogonalization) that hold non-target properties fixed, quantifying compute saved versus generation-based screening. Users and workflows: protein design, molecular optimization, and multi-property/constraint-satisfaction generative design, plus model evaluation and safety auditing of steered outputs.",
 "rw_append":
  " DELTA vs text-LLM pre-intervention side-effect prediction (Chalnev et al. 2024, SAE-Targeted Steering): they minimize side effects using effect vectors MEASURED empirically per target, whereas we predict the drift vector A PRIORI from fixed dictionary geometry (decoder overlaps + property-Jacobian) with NO generation, against HARD structural oracles. We claim a domain- and battery-specific empirical regularity, not a universal theory. Three separable contributions: (1) the finding (structured drift), (2) the predictor (geometry-derived signature), (3) the method (minimal-collateral controller); each is publishable on its own even if a later one fails.",
 "exp_edits": [
   ("500 generations per feature x strength level.",
    "500 generations per feature x strength level. SCOPE: the side-effect signature is computed PER FEATURE and predicts a drift VECTOR over the full property battery; we test whether it holds per model size, per SAE variant, and per domain, and report where it fails rather than asserting universality. All decisive thresholds below are reported with bootstrap confidence intervals over seeds."),
 ],
}

