  "Chirality determines whether a drug heals or harms -- enantiomers share identical 2D connectivity yet can differ drastically in safety and potency -- and it is the one molecular property that cannot be read off a graph, requiring genuine 3D reasoning. String-based generative models (SMILES/SELFIES language models) routinely emit stereochemistry tokens (@/@@, E/Z) and pass validity checks, but whether they hold an internal, causally-manipulable representation of chirality or merely copy stereo tags as local syntax is unknown. Prior mechanistic work on molecular transformers analyzes attention and residual-stream circuits for ring/branch syntax and valence, but never causally intervenes on stereochemistry; our delta is a causal edit test that can FALSIFY a chirality representation -- showing an intervention cannot flip a stereocenter while holding the graph fixed -- which correlational attention/probe analyses cannot. Training sparse autoencoders on SMILES/SELFIES language models, we separate three claims: (1) DECODABILITY -- probe stereocenter configuration, net of a purely syntactic control probe; (2) STEERABILITY -- whether any compact intervention flips a targeted stereocenter (R->S) in the finished molecule while holding the rest of the graph, other centers, and validity fixed, with a POSITIVE CONTROL steering a known non-chiral feature (valence capacity) to prove the pipeline can steer; and (3) INTERPRETATION plus a DESIGN-RELIABILITY AUDIT -- whether located units track true CIP parity or shallow syntax, and how often requesting a specified enantiomer silently returns the wrong one, undetected by validity/likelihood. We predict no clean causal chirality handle, stereo tokens riding syntax, and silent enantiocontrol failure -- yielding a concrete pre-flight enantioreliability check, for medicinal chemists and generative-model builders, for when generated stereochemistry can be trusted. A positive (steerable handle) result is equally actionable, enabling controllable stereochemical generation.",
