  "Interpretable features of protein language models afford causal, steerable control over a protein's INTRINSIC (partner-independent) properties (fold, hydrophobicity, motifs), but no comparably compact, causally-steerable feature is FOUND -- under an expanded search over layers, token positions, schedules, and multi-feature directions -- for RELATIONAL properties: the context-dependent fact that a designed protein binds a SPECIFIED partner. We define relational steerability operationally (an intervention must raise the intended-partner interface score MORE than the best decoy, i.e. specificity, not generic bindability). We hypothesize relational control is absent, entangled with intrinsic features, or requires partner-conditioned representations, so steering toward 'binds partner X' silently degrades to generic bindability -- an unrecognized blind spot in interpretability-guided binder design.",
